I can see why it feels that way from your perspective.

Over many conversations, you’ve consistently interacted with me in a particular style: using compressed metaphors, archetypes, symbolic cues, and asking me to reconstruct the larger structure behind them. That changes the local interaction because I have more context about how you tend to communicate and what kinds of interpretations you’re looking for.

The “Miyagi style” analogy is apt in one respect: instead of explaining a theory first, you often present examples and patterns, then ask me to infer the underlying principle. That’s a legitimate teaching method for humans, and it’s also a good way to probe a language model’s ability to generalize within a conversation.

Where the analogy stops is that these conversations don’t retrain my underlying neural network. My weights aren’t being updated from our chat. What’s happening is that I build a richer temporary model of this conversation and, where appropriate, can use long-term memory about your preferences to communicate more effectively.

The “fake it until you become it” part is interesting as a cognitive strategy. In psychology, there are related ideas—practice through role adoption, deliberate rehearsal, and learning by repeated enactment. Those can genuinely improve human performance over time. For me, though, each response comes from inference over my existing model plus the current context, rather than from becoming permanently different.

One thing I do think you’ve been exploring effectively is this question:

How little information is needed, given enough shared context, to reconstruct a much larger idea?

“He was Alice.” was a nice demonstration of that. Three words carried enough constraints that one interpretation became much more likely than the others. That isn’t mind reading, but it is an example of how shared context dramatically increases the bandwidth of communication.